paign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Sydney, Nova Scotia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.136389,"lo":-60.195556,"d":549,"ds":"Sydney is a former city and urban community on the east coast of Cape Breton Island in Nova Scotia, Canada within the Cape Breton Regional Municipality. Sydney was founded in 1785 by the British, incorporated as a city in 1904, and dissolved on 1 August 1995, when it was amalgamated into the regional municipality.","wpu":"Sydney, Nova Scotia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Sydney%2C_Nova_Scotia_skyline.jpg/960px-Sydney%2C_Nova_Scotia_skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Wentworth Park, Nova Scotia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.13216,"lo":-60.19307,"d":982,"ds":"Wentworth Park is a Canadian urban park located in the community of Sydney, part of Nova Scotia's Cape Breton Regional Municipality. The park was created in 1786 (1786), just a year after Sydney's founding, making it the oldest of Sydney's parks.","wpu":"Wentworth Park, Nova Scotia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Wentworth_Park_Arch.jpg/960px-Wentworth_Park_Arch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sydney Tar Ponds","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.15,"lo":-60.19,"d":1027,"ds":"The Sydney Tar Ponds were a hazardous waste site on Cape Breton Island in Nova Scotia, Canada. Located on the eastern shore of Sydney Harbour in the former city of Sydney (now amalgamated into the Cape Breton Regional Municipality), the Tar Ponds formed in a tidal estuary at the mouth of Muggah Creek, a freshwater stream that empties into the harbour.","wpu":"Sydney Tar Ponds","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Sydney_Tar_Pond_Nova_Scotia.jpeg/960px-Sydney_Tar_Pond_Nova_Scotia.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Temple Sons of Israel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.1323,"lo":-60.1843,"d":1176,"ds":"Temple Sons of Israel is a synagogue in Sydney, Nova Scotia. It is the only active synagogue in Cape Breton. The congregation was established in 1919.","wpu":"Temple Sons of Israel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Temple_Sons_of_Israel.jpg/960px-Temple_Sons_of_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Greenlink Rotary Park Trail System","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.12157,"lo":-60.181983,"d":2321,"ds":"Greenlink Rotary Park Trail System is a Canadian urban park located in the community of Sydney, part of Nova Scotia's Cape Breton Regional Municipality. The park incorporates the Greenlink Trail System, the Rotary Park Off Leash Park, as well as a disc golf course. Much of the park is forested, with ponds, a stone dam, and streams in the valleys; a few acres are open lawn area, suitable for informal recreation.","wpu":"Greenlink Rotary Park Trail System","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/42-20131022_Rotary_Park_GreenLink_Trails_0104.jpg/960px-42-20131022_Rotary_Park_GreenLink_Trails_0104.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"HMCS Protector","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.155,"lo":-60.220556,"d":2632,"ds":"Royal Canadian Navy base HMCS Protector, also known as the Point Edward Naval Base, was located next to Sydney Harbour, on Nova Scotia's Cape Breton Island. It was founded in 1940 and used by the navy during the Second World War, mainly to provision, protect and repair merchant marine convoys to Quebec, Halifax, and the United Kingdom. It was a main combat zone during the Battle of the St.","wpu":"HMCS Protector","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/HMCS_Protector_1943.jpg/960px-HMCS_Protector_1943.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sydney Harbour (Nova Scotia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.22,"lo":-60.205556,"d":8837,"ds":"Sydney Harbour (Mi'kmawi'simk: L'sipuktuk) is the 10-mile-long (16 km) Y-shaped inlet of the Atlantic, oriented southwest-northeast on the northeast shore of Cape Breton Island, Nova Scotia. At its upper reaches, the harbour forks to form two arms: the Northwest Arm and the South Arm. The South Arm is fed upstream by the Sydney River.","wpu":"Sydney Harbour (Nova Scotia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/20161001_Sydney_Harbour_%26_Queen_Mary_2.jpg/960px-20161001_Sydney_Harbour_%26_Queen_Mary_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Sydney is a former city and urban community on the east coast of Cape Breton Island in Nova Scotia, Canada within the Cape Breton Regional Municipality. Sydney was founded in 1785 by the British, incorporated as a city in 1904, and dissolved on 1 August 1995, when it was amalgamated into the regional municipality. Sydney served as Cape Breton Island's colonial capital until 1820, when the colony was annexed to Nova Scotia.","wpu":"Sydney, Nova Scotia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Sydney%2C_Nova_Scotia_skyline.jpg/960px-Sydney%2C_Nova_Scotia_skyline.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}